We need to throw away the climbing AI for a second time. It does not work to get to a solution iteratively. We have to plan properly how that climbing behavior is supposed to work. We need to create a plan file that describes the climbing behavior in detail, including the mechanics of how the player interacts with the wall and anchors.

We assume that at start the player is attached with all legs and hands to the wall to respective anchors. How climbing usually works is that you free up a leg from its anchor which then flexes at hip and knee to reach a higher up anchor. That anchor should not be too close to the lowest hand anchor. Otherwise the body would move further away from the wall which would require more strength to hold on to the anchors. So in that case it would be better to move forward with the lowest hand/arm and flex that to find an anchor above the head to grab that. 

It is important that the legs keep a certain distance to the hands so that the body does not move too far away from the wall. The player should always try to keep the center of mass close to the wall to maintain balance and stability. This ensures that the legs do not overtake the hands as well.

Back to the regular case where the target anchor for the free leg is not too close to the lowest hand anchor. Once the leg reached the target anchor it latches there and releases the other leg from its anchor. Then the new latched leg goes into extension (hip and knee) to push up the player. The next step should be to free up the lowest hand/arm and reach for a higher anchor above the head if possible. The arm is then in extension to pull the body up later together with the leg extension of the now free flexed leg.

The player should not move too far away from the wall to avoid losing grip on the anchors. The climbing motion should be smooth and continuous, with each limb moving in coordination with the others to maintain balance and stability.

The entire climbing is driving by the spring based skeleton. That means the entire motion is driven by target angles the joints are supposed to hold. For a smooth motion these angles have to be animated over time. The Inverse Kinematic has to be computed for a leg or arm to flex or extend respectively. This way target anchors can be reached that are within arm or leg reach of the body joint to the anchor. It may happen that during a push up by extension of the leg that the latched arms are changing their shoulder/elbow angles because the relative position of the entire player changed relative to the arms latched anchor. For example an arm in extension needs to go into flex now to allow the push up. The skeleton has to adapt to this to avoid the physics fighting to push the player down against what the legs do. This also makes sense relative to real climbing where pushing up with the legs will change the position of the arms relative to the wall and the anchors but also release the strength needed hold on to the anchors with the hands/arms.

The entire thing should follow the TDD or BDD principles, where we define the expected behavior and outcomes before implementing the climbing logic. This will help ensure that the climbing AI behaves as intended and can be tested effectively. The game should run with a random Wall.ts.noiseIndex so that the world is different each time. The tests should use a fixed set of wall.ts.noiseIndex to ensure consistent and repeatable results. 
